The holy Lord said:
Listen, O Goddess, I shall explain what you ask of me;
by merely hearing it, one becomes dear to the yoginīs.

the pair 'gṛhṇa', the word 'ruru', then the word 'kṣetrapāla';
one should utter 'sarvopacārasahitāmimāṃ pūjāṃ baliṃ',
'gṛhṇa gṛhṇa' ending with two ṭhas (svāhā)—this is the mantra of the guardian of the sacred field, O dear one.

In the west [one should present the offering to] the god Baṭuka, in the north the offering to the yoginīs,
in the east one should give the offering to the spirits, and the guardian of the sacred field in the south;
one should worship Rājarājeśvara in the center, O leader of Kula.

The offering to the spirits is proclaimed with all the fingers, O dear one;
the offering to the guardian of the sacred field is made with the thumb and forefinger,
and that of Rājarājeśvara with the thumb and middle finger.

For your devotees endowed with the seventh stage, there is great fruit;
the eight convictions arising from knowledge of the three times, O Mistress of Kula,
and the eight states such as trembling arise—of this there is no doubt.

The glorious Goddess said:
O Lord of Kula, I wish to hear about yoga and the characteristics of the lord of yogins;
tell me the fruit of worship through devotion to Kula, O ocean of compassion!

The Lord said:
Listen, O Goddess, I shall explain what you ask of me;
by the mere hearing of it, yoga directly reveals itself.

The Lord said:
Listen, Goddess, I shall explain that which you ask me;
Merely by hearing it, devotion quickly arises.

The Lord said:
Listen, O Devi, I shall tell you that which you ask of me;
by merely hearing it, purification of the mind arises.

Those men who are devoid of devotion at first, but devoted in the middle,
and whose devotion becomes greatly increased at the end, are fit at the end;
and they are designated as possessing superior knowledge. Thus instruction is threefold, O beloved.
